body:
Derive the equation for time evolution using the backward (implicit) Euler method. We want to calculate

$$
\begin{align}
r_{i+1} &= r_i + dt \cdot v_{i+1} \\
v_{i+1} &= v_i + dt \cdot f_{i+1}
\end{align}
$$

but it is not possible to describe $f_{i+1}$ analytically at this stage.  
(Note that if we assume $m=1$, $dv_i/dt = a_i = f_i/m = f_i$.)

Therefore, considering the Taylor expansion of $f_{i+1}$, we have $v_{i+1}$ as follows.

$$
\begin{align}
v_{i+1} &= v_i + dt \cdot f_{i+1} \\
&= v_i + dt \cdot \left[ f_i + \left.\frac{df}{dr}\right|_ {r=r_i} (r_{i+1} - r_i) \right]
\end{align}
$$

Here, summarizing the terms for step $i+1$ in above equations to the left hand side, we obtain

$$
\begin{align}
r_{i+1} - dt \cdot v_{i+1} &= r_i \\
v_{i+1} - dt \cdot \left.\frac{df}{dr}\right|_ {r=r_i} \cdot r_{i+1} &= v_i + dt \cdot f_i - dt \cdot \left.\frac{df}{dr}\right|_ {r=r_i} \cdot r_i
\end{align}
$$

Expressing the coefficients on the left-hand side as a matrix, 

$$
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & -dt \\\
-dt \cdot \left.\frac{df}{dr}\right|_ {r=r_i} & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
r_{i+1} \\\
v_{i+1}
\end{bmatrix}
=\begin{bmatrix}
r_i \\\
v_i + dt \cdot f_i - dt \cdot \left.\frac{df}{dr}\right|_ {r=r_i} \cdot r_i
\end{bmatrix}
$$

That is, letting $A$ be the left-hand side coefficient matrix and $b$ be the right-hand side vector, the value at step $i+1$ can be solved as

$$
\begin{bmatrix}
r_{i+1} \\\
v_{i+1}
\end{bmatrix} = A^{-1}b
$$

According to the above derivation, in the program, the appropriate expressions were given as entries of matrix $A$ and vector $b$.
